{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789321340,"nonce":"jRgKe1sRxWCSri1CrqJrCNnnRhI=","title":"Library string builders were quadratic: join, repr and per-character loops","message":"A `\"\".join(parts)` over a million one-character strings brought the machine down. `zb_str_join` built its result as `out = out + sep; out = out + part`, one fresh string per step and the previous one never released (see the drop issue), so the total allocation was the sum of every intermediate length: about 500 GB for a 1 MB result. The list and dict reprs, `repr(str)`, `str.title`, a stepped slice and `split()` had the same shape, and every per-character loop indexed the string with `$String$char_at`, which validates and walks the UTF-8 from the start on each call, so they were quadratic in time as well.\n\nNow:\n- `$String$join_n(data, n, sep)` in the string plugin joins the strings a `List\u003cString\u003e`'s storage points at in one allocation; `zb_str_join` calls it with the list's `data` and `len`.\n- The reprs, `repr(str)`, `title` and the stepped slice gather pieces in a `List\u003cString\u003e` and join once.\n- `$String$char_at_byte`, `$String$next_byte` and `$String$bytes` are a constant-time cursor over the characters by byte offset; `zb_str_chars` walks it, `split()` cuts words out by byte offset, and the per-character builders index the character list instead of the string.\n\nMeasured: the million-part join peaks at 144 MB and takes 0.13 s; `repr` of a million-int list, a 1.2 MB string, `title` and `[::3]` over a megabyte each run in 0.1 to 0.3 s.\n\nLeft as is: the `dict` repr of 100k entries takes 90 s, all of it in building the dict (12f1439).","files":null}]}